  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is centered over Southern California and \nArizona and extends over western portions of Nebraska and Kansas. \nIncreased lift from this upper trough has resulted in rain showers \nacross western portions of Kansas and western and central portions \nof Nebraska. A cold front is moving through the forecast area with \ngusty, northerly winds behind it. High temperatures today across the \nforecast area are expected to range from the low 60s to around 80 \ndegrees. The rain showers are primarily expected to move across far \nwestern and northern portions of the area today and tonight. Winds \nwill weaken this evening into tonight (to around 5 MPH) out of the \nnortheast. Low temperatures tonight are expected to range from the \nmid 30s to mid 50s. \n\nThe center of the upper trough will get closer to the region on \nSunday with upper lift increasing across the area. Development of \nshowers and some thunderstorms (non-severe) is expected to increase \nmid Sunday afternoon into Sunday evening moving in from the \nsouthwest. High temperatures on Sunday may be slightly cooler than \nthose today with the cold front still bisecting the forecast area. \nThe rain and possibly some storms will continue through Sunday night \nwith low temperatures ranging from the low 40s to upper 50s. The \nupper trough will be over the region on Monday with high \ntemperatures generally in the 60s and low 70s. Rain and storms are \nstill expected Monday into Monday night but will briefly come to an \nend on Tuesday before more chances (15% to 30%) return Tuesday \nevening. A surface high will be across the area Monday night with \nlow temperatures expected to range from the low 30s to low 50s. The \nsurface high will still be across the area on Tuesday with high \ntemperatures similar to the previous day. \n\nTemperatures Wednesday and Thursday will be on a warming trend with \nhighs on Thursday and Friday mostly in the 70s. There is some \nuncertainty with temperatures, especially Thursday and Friday with \nan approaching upper trough and increasing rain and storm chances \n(15% to around 40%).",
